  },
  "question": {
    "text": "A coil is placed in a magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math> as shown below :<br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0074_6958d44e58ef.png\" style=\"width: 348px; height: 160px;\" /><br />A current is induced in the coil because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math> is :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "parallel to the plane of coil and increasing with time",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "outward and increasing with time",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "outward and decreasing with time",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "parallel to the plane of coil and decreasing with time",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Lenz&#39;s&nbsp;law state that the direction of the induced current is such that it opposes its cause of generation, so,&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0074_5cbc2b8d02e8.png\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 196px;\" /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>,&nbsp;must not be parallel to the plane of coil for non-zero flux and according to Lenz&#39;s&nbsp;law if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is outward it should be decreasing for anticlockwise induced current.</p>"
  }
}